{"kind":"request","text":"SB-1 pre-code audit, main 694a8c7f83deedb4e8d34706c51203029726121a and AE-1 draft 9927d5a9de6ff4d9eea21925c5aeeaf5e1b69a09. Source only. No implementation or executable validation.\n\n1. Writes and bounds.\nThe sole production SQLite scope writer is packages/scope/src/sqlite.ts:586-594, SqliteStore.setScope. It reads the previous row at587 and INSERT/UPDATEs canonicalize(scope) at588 without encoded-size or held-count checks. scope() at321 selects the complete row; JSON.parse at150-151 checks neither shape nor bytes. All other generic meta writers use other fixed keys.\n\nThe sole production derive call is packages/derive/src/fold.ts:323, reached by the owning turn at packages/scope/src/turn.ts:533-537. The entry byte guard at515-516 uses entryBytes262144 (packages/contract/src/bounds.ts:109), not4096. Fold constructs the eight scope fields. It initializes held from provisional-genesis nonzero send ordinals and preserves prior held at97; confirmation clears held at168. ScopeState.held has no length ceiling (packages/derive/src/state.ts:20-29). MemoryState.setScope at596, replay/src/view.ts:85 and sqlite.ts:554 only write/copy memory projections.\n\nClean rows under normal PROPOSED_BOUNDS104 sends (contract/src/bounds.ts:111), canonical20/24-character time (bytes/src/ids.ts:60-74), closed kinds/references and safe integer ordinals appear comfortably below4096. Independent source review found no clean-default counterexample. This is not an unconditional write invariant: object.ts:113 exposes Wiring.bounds and205 selects it without a104 ceiling; setScope accepts a type-correct ScopeState with arbitrarily many integer held elements.10000 held integers alone exceed4096. That is a write-API counterexample, not a claim that clean default ingress creates it. I do not propose closing SB-1 with a static normal-default fixture.\n\n2. Recovery for preparation-refused metadata.\nnativeBirth at object.ts:810 calls #recoverArchiveAlarm before deadline/PreparationBudget at811-814. After diagnostics, recovery reads and parses the complete row at450 even without a restart directive. A parseable oversized active row may receive a deployment restart. Regardless of status, recovery calls ArchiveDriver.wake at452; archive-driver.ts:122 -\u003e #store at102 -\u003e current() -\u003e object-archive.ts:21 store.scope() reads it again. Recovery then awaits getAlarm at455, calls wake again at460, and Wakes.set at461/outbox.ts:81-89 invokes archive wake once more. Thus oversized parseable metadata may rearm a missing/later alarm or change restart scheduling metadata, without provider/native work or head change, before preparation refuses.\n\nPreparation uses PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES=4096 (preparation-scope.ts:9), charged before SQL CASE at16-17; over-bound/non-string/malformed metadata becomes unavailable at20-31. ReadPreparation returns null at read-preparation.ts:87-88; native-birth.ts:61-62 returns null. An in-bound scope can also recover an alarm before a later proof/pin refusal; preserve that. Malformed JSON at object.ts:450 can throw synchronously outside the async catch at462. Async scheduling failure is caught without changing the read's answer; ArchiveDriver.wake catches its own failure and may return its fallback/retry.\n\nAE-1 preserves this closure: its recovery lines441-469 equal main plus a two-line import shift; object-archive.ts, sqlite.ts, preparation-scope.ts and outbox.ts are unchanged. AE policy/terminal changes affect ArchiveDriver.pass, not current()/wake. Preserve its terminal scheduling semantics.\n\nProposed route after AE-1: use PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES with no duplicate4096 constant. A recovery-only metadata/status read must withhold over-bound bytes in SQL before transfer/parsing and distinguish absent/unavailable from an in-bound status. Unavailable metadata returns before restart/wake/alarm lookup. Recheck after awaited getAlarm before wake/Wakes.set, because an intervening event can change metadata. Keep active-only restart, status-independent in-bound wake recovery, revision/earlier-alarm arbitration, ordinary scope() and existing preparation answers.\n\nWitness plan: extend packages/scope/test/archive-driver.test.ts:90-137, whose real ScopeObject peer read already proves future/due rearming, earlier-alarm preservation, unchanged archive row/head and zero host work. Add oversized metadata: preparation-null, missing alarm, actual peer read, joined scheduled callbacks, no rearm/restart/archive mutation/provider work. Observe SQL returned cells so transferring the oversized row fails the witness; null alone proves too little. Add getAlarm held while metadata crosses from in-bound to oversized, and in-bound refused/provisional statuses preserving wake recovery.\n\nPlanner question before source: constructor activation also belongs to the peer-call closure. object.ts:351 owns a separately bounded diagnostic (archive-operator.ts:35-46, ARCHIVE_SCOPE_METADATA_BYTES16384 at archive-pin.ts:9). Production activation at object.ts:372-378 independently calls unbounded store.scope(), genesis(), committed() and Wakes.set; genesis() at235-238 reads stored genesis. Signer/credential activation at354-364 can also invoke shared archive wake. A guard only in #recoverArchiveAlarm cannot establish the literal peer-call closure claim across activation. May SB-1 guard these archive scheduling activation continuations/current callbacks with PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES too, retaining the separately bounded diagnostic? Or is its claim deliberately limited to #recoverArchiveAlarm? I recommend including that archive scheduling closure without unrelated native-turn/API changes. No code; SB-1 follows AE-1 and does not hold it.","body":{"conditions":"Resolve activation closure scope and accept/correct bounded route and witness plan before source. SB-1 code remains after AE-1.","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:836283176f960b8c886eeab41ee36ca4b83316e3 stal